course: world
lesson: 101
title: Reading the clock: hours, minutes and the two hands
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A clock with hands is like a race track with two runners: the long hand races all the way round once every hour, and the short hand creeps round only once every twelve hours.
explain: The short hand tells the hour and the long hand tells the minutes. When the long hand points straight up at 12, it is exactly "o'clock": short hand on 7, long hand on 12, and Mum yells up the stairs, "It's seven, shift it, you'll miss brekkie!" ("shift it" means hurry up, "brekkie" means breakfast).
explain: The clock face is a circle cut into 12 equal parts. For the long hand each number is worth 5 minutes, so you count in fives: on 3 it is 15 minutes past, on 6 it is 30, on 8 it is 40, and all the way round is 60.
explain: The short hand slides slowly between the numbers as the hour goes by. At half past four it sits halfway between 4 and 5, and at 9:50 it is almost on the 10, but the hour is still 9 until the long hand reaches 12.
explain: Many clocks also have a thin, fast second hand that goes round once a minute, and old or fancy clocks may write their numbers as Roman numerals, where I is 1, V is 5 and X is 10.
fact: On a clock with hands, the short hand shows the hour and the long hand shows the minutes.
fact: When the long hand points at 12, the time is exactly o'clock.
fact: For the long hand, each number on the clock face stands for 5 minutes, so the long hand on 8 means 40 minutes past (8 × 5 = 40).
fact: There are 60 minutes in an hour, and 12 numbers × 5 minutes = 60.
fact: The long hand on 3 means quarter past, 15 minutes after the hour; on 6 it means half past, 30 minutes after the hour.
fact: In Britain and Ireland "half four" is short for half past four, 4:30; it does not mean half of four.
fact: During the hour the short hand moves slowly towards the next number, so at 9:50 it is nearly on 10 while the hour is still 9.
fact: A digital clock shows the hour before the colon and the minutes after it, so 06:05 is five past six; the 0 in front just fills the space.
fact: When the long hand is past 6, people often count the minutes "to" the next hour: the long hand on 11 means five to the hour.
fact: The thin second hand goes round once every 60 seconds, which is 1 minute.
fact: On a Roman clock I = 1, V = 5 and X = 10, so III is 3, VI is 6, IX is 9 and XII is 12; some clocks show 4 as IIII because clockmakers liked how it looked.
fact: On a clock face the 12 numbers are spread evenly: 12 at the top, 3 on the right, 6 at the bottom and 9 on the left.
fact: Mixing up the two hands is the most common clock mistake: always find the short hand first for the hour.
fact: Words people use: "innit" means "isn't it" (London), "no cap" means "no lie, honestly", "sus" means suspicious or fishy, "wee" means small (Scotland), and "pet" and "love" are friendly words for anyone in parts of England.
exercise: Which hand of a clock shows the hour? | The short hand | The long hand | The thin second hand | Whichever hand is on top
exercise: Leo sees the short hand on 7 and the long hand on 12. What time is it? | Seven o'clock | Twelve past seven | Five to twelve | Twelve o'clock
exercise: Ava says it is 3:00 because the long hand is on 3, but the short hand is just past 12. What is the real time? | 12:15, quarter past twelve | 3:00, three o'clock | 3:12, twelve past three | 12:03, three past twelve
exercise: How many minutes is each number worth for the long hand? | 5 minutes | 1 minute | 10 minutes | 12 minutes
exercise: The long hand is on 8. How many minutes past the hour is it? | 40 minutes | 8 minutes | 20 minutes | 45 minutes
exercise: Why are there 60 minutes all the way round the clock? | 12 numbers × 5 minutes = 60 | 6 numbers × 10 minutes = 60 | Clocks have 60 big numbers on them | The short hand goes round 60 times
exercise: Grandad's watch: short hand halfway between 4 and 5, long hand on 6. "Half four, love," he says. What time is it? | 4:30 | 2:00 | 3:30 | 4:15
exercise: Where is the short hand at half past four? | Halfway between 4 and 5 | Exactly on the 4 | Exactly on the 5 | On the 6, next to the long hand
exercise: The long hand is on 10 and the short hand is almost on 10. What time is it? | 9:50 | 10:50 | 10:10 | 9:10
exercise: Finn's digital watch says 06:05. What is another way to say it? | Five past six | Six past five | Five to six | Half past six
exercise: What does the 0 in 06:05 do? | It just fills the space in front of a single number | It shows that the time is at night | It shows that the watch is broken | It means no hours have passed today
exercise: In class the long hand is on 11 and the short hand is nearly on 3. What time is it? | Five to three, 2:55 | Eleven past three, 3:11 | Five past eleven, 11:05 | Five past three, 3:05
exercise: How long does the second hand take to go once all the way round? | 1 minute | 1 hour | 12 hours | 1 second
exercise: Which Roman numeral means 9? | IX | XI | VIIII | VI
exercise: On Nan's Roman clock, what number does XII stand for? | 12 | 7 | 102 | 21
exercise: Where does the 3 go on a clock face? | On the right side | At the very top | At the bottom | On the left side
exercise: Amira squeezes all 12 numbers onto the top half of her clock. What goes wrong? | The hands can no longer point to the right times | The clock will run twice as fast | Only the seconds will be wrong | Nothing, as long as they are in order
test: Tyler goes, "long hand's on the 3, so it's 3 o'clock, no cap." The short hand is just past the 12. Is he right? | No, it's quarter past twelve; the short hand gives the hour | Yes, the long hand always gives the hour | Yes, but only in the afternoon | No, it's three minutes past twelve
test: Mia's cousin from America hears "half four" and asks, "Half of four, so two o'clock?" What does it really mean? | Half past four, 4:30 | Two o'clock, 2:00 | Half past three, 3:30 | Quarter past four, 4:15
test: Zara: "It's nearly ten innit, so call it 10:50." The long hand is on 10 and the short hand is almost on 10. What's the time? | 9:50, because the hour stays 9 until the long hand reaches 12 | 10:50, because the short hand is on the 10 | 10:10, because both hands point at the 10 | 10:00, because nearly ten means ten o'clock
test: Ruby: "Dad, the clock's broken, the skinny hand is zooming round!" What is really going on? | That is the second hand; it goes round once a minute | The clock is broken; no hand should move that fast | It is the hour hand on fast-forward | It is the minute hand, so the clock is running fast
test: Jack moans, "Nan's clock is well sus, it's got letters, not numbers." What is true? | They are Roman numerals, numbers written with letters | It is only a decoration and cannot tell time | The letters are the names of the twelve hours | It is a clock made for people of another language
test: What would happen if a clock's two hands were exactly the same length? | You could not tell which hand shows the hour | The clock would run twice as fast | It would show only o'clock times | It would show seconds instead of minutes
test: In a cartoon, a teacher counts the numbers 1 to 8 to the long hand and says, "Eight minutes past!" Put the cartoon right. | It is 40 minutes past, because each number is 5 minutes | It is 8 minutes past, because each number is 1 minute | It is 80 minutes past, because each number is 10 minutes | It is 16 minutes past, because each number is 2 minutes
test: A wee Scottish lad in class: "Big hand's on the 11, wee hand's near the 3. Home time at 3, aye?" How long till home time? | 5 minutes; it is five to three | None; it is three o'clock already | Hours; it is only eleven o'clock | 11 minutes; it is 2:49
test: Finn reads 06:05 as "six oh five". His mum says, "Five past six, yeah." Who is right? | Both of them; it is the same time | Only Mum; "six oh five" is wrong | Only Finn; five past six is 5:06 | Neither; the time is five to six
test: A phone game shows the long hand on 12 and the short hand on 3 and asks, "Is it 12:03?" | No, it is three o'clock | Yes, three minutes past twelve | No, it is quarter past twelve | No, it is twelve minutes past three
test: Big brother, fed up: "Shut up and read it. Long hand on the 6, short hand between 1 and 2. What's the time?" | Half past one | Half past two | Six minutes past one | Half past six
exam: Identify which hand of an analogue clock, that is, a clock with hands, indicates the hour. | The shorter hand | The longer hand | The thinnest hand | The hand indicating the twelve
exam: It is given that the long hand points to the numeral eight. Ascertain the number of minutes elapsed since the hour. | Forty minutes | Eight minutes | Twenty minutes | Forty-eight minutes
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the expression "half four" as employed in Britain and Ireland. | It denotes thirty minutes past four o'clock | It denotes two o'clock precisely | It denotes thirty minutes past three o'clock | It denotes fifteen minutes past four o'clock
exam: Determine the interval required for the second hand to complete one revolution of the dial. | Sixty seconds | Sixty minutes | Twelve seconds | Twelve hours
